            href="https://www.buildinggreen.com/feature/using-smart-glass-smart"
            data-gtm-vis-has-fired7025260_75="1"><img
              class="img-fluid mt10 border w-100"
              src="/sites/default/files/is-using-smart-glass-smart-spotlight-report.jpg"
              data-gtm-vis-has-fired7025260_75="1"></a></div>

        <div class="col-sm-9" data-gtm-vis-has-fired7025260_75="1">
          <h4 data-gtm-vis-has-fired7025260_75="1"><a
              href="https://www.buildinggreen.com/feature/using-smart-glass-smart"
              data-gtm-vis-has-fired7025260_75="1">Is Using Smart Glass
              Smart?</a></h4>

          <p data-gtm-vis-has-fired7025260_75="1">The sun streaming into our buildings can be both a benefit and a
            nuisance. Controlling its glare and solar heat gain in order to maximize energy efficiency, occupant
            comfort, and views to the outdoors is difficult. In commercial offices and all-glass buildings, the impacts
            are multiplied. Low-e glazing and exterior or interior shading systems are most often used to manage
            daylight, but there is another option—electrochromic glazing, sometimes called smart glass, switchable
            glass, dynamic glazing, and other names (in this report, we’re going to call it “smart glass” and are not
            covering thermochromic and other light-blocking glazing technologies).</p>

          <p data-gtm-vis-has-fired7025260_75="1">With smart glass, you can make windows clear or tinted on demand,
            offering the promise of better comfort and significant energy savings without blinds or louvers obscuring
            the view. But early versions of the technology had one big problem; the color of the light through the glass
