            key turning point in segregating our cities, creating the maps we have today. In fact, it all began with
            maps—maps released during the New Deal era by the Federal Housing Agency (FHA). They indicated which
            neighborhoods were appropriate for mortgages and which were not, and they overtly discriminated against
            black citizens by refusing to guarantee loans for homes in the “wrong” neighborhoods. The practice is called
            “redlining” because the black neighborhoods were delineated in red.</p>

